well another group of clark county  school district students returned to  campus today we're talking about cohort  b  they started in-person learning under  the hybrid instruction model  8 news now reporter kristen drummond is  live in the newsroom with how families  and students adjusted  kristen well denise roughly 20 000  students went in buildings for the first  time in nearly a year  they are the other half of the more than  40 000 pre-kindergarten to third grade  students  attending class in person this week  around the valley now cohort b follows  the same plan as cohort a  two days of in-person learning and three  days of distance education  except cohort b comes on campuses  thursday and friday  instead of monday and tuesday now  various families and ccsd  staff say the overall transition is  going well like it did earlier this week  the schedule is a minor adjustment but  some parents tell me their children  seem to be excited to return she was so  happy  a little nervous but we got to the bus  stop and at least one friend was there  so she felt better they both got on the  bus together  now here are the latest cohort numbers  from ccsd  nearly 22 000 in cohort a roughly 20 000  in cohort b  and more than 50 000 in cohort c which  is full-time distance education  the data from ccsd shows a couple  hundred students are no longer doing  full-time distance education  it's likely those children switch to  hybrid learning this week since  both cohorts a and b gained more  students according to the numbers now  that's a trend the district does expect  to continue  happening the rest of this school year  elementary schools are starting to also  focus on preparing for  in-person instruction five days a week  that starts on april 6  for pre-kindergarten to fifth grade  full-time distance education remains an  option though for families  who prefer remote learning reporting  live in the newsroom kristin drummond  8 news now kristin thank you for that
 

